<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Why Is the Sky Blue? · 天空为什么是蓝色的？</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --accent: #1d6fd1;
    --ink: #22303e;
    --muted: #5b6b7c;
    --card: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.94);
    --line: #dde8f4;
    --l1: #f2913d;
    --l2: #2e86de;
    --l3: #6c3fc5;
  }
  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
  html, body { height: 100%; overflow: hidden; }
  body {
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue",
      Arial, "PingFang SC", "Hiragino Sans GB", "Microsoft YaHei", "Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: linear-gradient(165deg, #cfe9ff 0%, #8ec6f5 45%, #4a90d9 100%);
  }

  /* ---------- progress bar ---------- */
  #progress {
    position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; height: 4px; width: 0;
    background: var(--accent); border-radius: 0 2px 2px 0;
    transition: width .4s ease; z-index: 20;
  }

  /* ---------- decorative sky ---------- */
  .sun {
    position: absolute; top: 7%; right: 8%; font-size: 64px; z-index: 0;
    animation: bob 5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  @keyframes bob { 0%,100% { transform: translateY(0); } 50% { transform: translateY(12px); } }
  .cloud {
    position: absolute; height: 34px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.85);
    border-radius: 40px; z-index: 0; animation: drift linear infinite;
  }
  .cloud::before, .cloud::after { content: ""; position: absolute; background: inherit; border-radius: 50%; }
  .cloud::before { width: 54px; height: 54px; top: -25px; left: 16px; }
  .cloud::after  { width: 38px; height: 38px; top: -15px; right: 16px; }
  @keyframes drift { from { transform: translateX(-25vw); } to { transform: translateX(125vw); } }
  .c1 { top: 12%; width: 120px; animation-duration: 75s; }
  .c2 { top: 30%; width: 90px;  animation-duration: 100s; animation-delay: -40s; opacity: .8; }
  .c3 { top: 70%; width: 70px;  animation-duration: 130s; animation-delay: -70s; opacity: .55; }

  /* ---------- deck / slides ---------- */
  #deck { position: relative; height: 100%; }
  .slide {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 1;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    padding: 44px 24px 96px;
    opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; transform: translateX(48px);
    transition: opacity .45s ease, transform .45s ease, visibility .45s;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }
  .slide.active { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; transform: none; }

  .card {
    width: 100%; max-width: 1180px;
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: 20px;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(23, 74, 135, .28);
    padding: 28px 34px 34px;
  }

  /* ---------- title slide ---------- */
  .title-card { text-align: center; max-width: 760px; padding: 48px 36px; }
  .title-card .en-big { font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.3rem); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.5px; color: var(--accent); }
  .title-card .cn-big { font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4.2vw, 2.7rem); font-weight: 700; margin-top: 10px; color: var(--ink); }
  .title-card .rule { width: 64px; height: 4px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--accent); margin: 26px auto; opacity: .5; }
  .title-card p { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--muted); }
  .title-card p.cn { margin-top: 6px; }

  /* ---------- two columns ---------- */
  .cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .col { padding: 6px 30px; min-width: 0; }
  .col:first-child { padding-left: 4px; }
  .col:last-child  { padding-right: 4px; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .col > h2 {
    font-size: 1.28rem; font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 14px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
  }
  .col > h2 .dot { width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%; flex: none; }
  .l1 .dot { background: var(--l1); } .l2 .dot { background: var(--l2); } .l3 .dot { background: var(--l3); }
  .l1 h2 { color: var(--l1); } .l2 h2 { color: var(--l2); } .l3 h2 { color: var(--l3); }

  .lang-tag {
    display: inline-block; font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em;
    color: var(--accent); background: #e8f2fd; border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 3px 9px; margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .col p { font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.75; margin: 10px 0; }
  .col h3 { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 700; margin: 18px 0 6px; color: var(--ink); }
  .col ul, .col ol { margin: 10px 0 10px 22px; font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .col li { margin: 7px 0; }
  .col code {
    font-family: "SF Mono", ui-monospace, Consolas, "Courier New", monospace;
    font-size: .86em; background: #eef4fb; border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .formula {
    display: block; text-align: center;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    background: #f2f7fd; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0; font-size: 1.02rem; letter-spacing: .3px;
  }
  .tldr {
    margin-top: 18px; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 10px;
    background: #f6f9fe; border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: .93rem; line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* ---------- controls ---------- */
  #controls {
    position: fixed; bottom: 18px; left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%);
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; z-index: 30;
    background: rgba(255,255,255,.88); border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 9px 18px; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(23,74,135,.25);
  }
  #controls button {
    border: 0; cursor: pointer; background: var(--accent); color: #fff;
    width: 34px; height: 34px; border-radius: 50%; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    transition: background .2s, opacity .2s;
  }
  #controls button:hover:not(:disabled) { background: #155bb0; }
  #controls button:disabled { opacity: .35; cursor: default; }
  .dots { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; }
  .dots button {
    width: 10px; height: 10px; padding: 0; background: #b9d4ef; border-radius: 999px;
  }
  .dots button.active { width: 24px; background: var(--accent); }
  .dots button:hover:not(.active) { background: #8fbbe4; }
  #counter { font-size: .8rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--muted); min-width: 44px; text-align: center; }
  #hint {
    position: fixed; bottom: 22px; right: 22px; z-index: 30;
    font-size: .74rem; color: rgba(255,255,255,.9);
    background: rgba(29,74,135,.35); border-radius: 999px; padding: 5px 12px;
    backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
  }
  #fs {
    position: fixed; top: 16px; right: 18px; z-index: 30;
    border: 0; cursor: pointer; background: rgba(255,255,255,.85); color: var(--accent);
    width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 16px;
    box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(23,74,135,.2);
  }
  #fs:hover { background: #fff; }

  /* ---------- responsive ---------- */
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .col { padding: 10px 6px; }
    .col:last-child { border-left: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); margin-top: 8px; }
    .card { padding: 22px 20px 26px; }
    #hint { display: none; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    * { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="progress"></div>
<div class="sun" aria-hidden="true">☀️</div>
<div class="cloud c1" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="cloud c2" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="cloud c3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<button id="fs" title="Toggle fullscreen" aria-label="Toggle fullscreen">⛶</button>

<div id="deck">

  <!-- ============ SLIDE 1 · Title ============ -->
  <section class="slide" data-label="Title">
    <div class="card title-card">
      <h1 class="en-big" lang="en">Why Is the Sky Blue?</h1>
      <h1 class="cn-big" lang="zh-Hans">天空为什么是蓝色的？</h1>
      <div class="rule"></div>
      <p lang="en">Three explanations of the same phenomenon, tuned for three audiences.</p>
      <p class="cn" lang="zh-Hans">同一个现象的三种解释，分别面向三种不同的听众。</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SLIDE 2 · Level 1 ============ -->
  <section class="slide l1" data-label="Level 1 · 3岁幼儿">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col" lang="en">
          <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>Level 1: A 3-Year-Old</h2>
          <p>Sunshine looks white, but it's actually all the colors of a rainbow mixed together! When the sunlight hits the air all around us, the air bounces it around in every direction — like a splash of water. Blue bounces around the most, so when you look up, you see lots and lots of blue all over the sky!</p>
          <p>And when the sun is about to go to bed, its light has to travel through way more air. So the blue bounces away, and only the red and orange make it to your eyes. That's why the sky glows orange at sunset. 🌅</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col" lang="zh-Hans">
          <span class="lang-tag">中文</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>第一级：3岁幼儿</h2>
          <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但它其实是彩虹里所有颜色混在一起的！当阳光照到周围的空气上时，空气会把光向四面八方弹开——就像水花溅开一样。蓝色被弹开得最多，所以当你抬头看天空时，就会看到整个天空都满满的都是蓝色！</p>
          <p>太阳快要“睡觉”的时候，它的光要穿过厚得多的空气。于是蓝色全被弹走了，只有红色和橙色能到达你的眼睛。这就是为什么日落时天空会泛着橙色。🌅</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SLIDE 3 · Level 2 ============ -->
  <section class="slide l2" data-label="Level 2 · 高中生">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col" lang="en">
          <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>Level 2: A High School Teenager</h2>
          <p><strong>The key idea:</strong> the Sun emits white light — a mix of all visible colors — and the atmosphere scatters the short (blue) wavelengths out of the beam much more than the long (red) ones.</p>

          <h3>1. Scattering by air molecules</h3>
          <p>When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it interacts with nitrogen and oxygen molecules. These molecules are far smaller than the wavelength of light, so they act like tiny antennas: the light's electric field shakes their electrons, and the shaken electrons radiate light in all directions. This is <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</p>
          <p>The amount scattered follows an approximate <strong>1/λ⁴ law</strong> — halve the wavelength and you scatter ~16× more light. So blue light (~450 nm) is scattered roughly <code>(650/450)⁴ ≈ 4×</code> more strongly than red (~650 nm). When you look anywhere in the sky away from the Sun, you're seeing mostly blue light that was scattered <em>into</em> your line of sight by the air above you.</p>

          <h3>2. Why blue, not violet?</h3>
          <p>Violet (~400 nm) is actually scattered even more strongly than blue. Three things conspire:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The Sun emits less violet</strong> (and much less UV) than green or yellow — the solar spectrum peaks near 500 nm.</li>
            <li><strong>Your eyes are barely sensitive to violet</strong> — photopic sensitivity drops to a few percent by 450 nm and under 1% below 400 nm.</li>
            <li><strong>Ozone absorbs UV</strong> (Hartley band below ~320 nm), removing the shortest wavelengths before they reach you.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The net result of all the scattered light reaching your eye is perceived as blue.</p>

          <h3>3. Red sunsets</h3>
          <p>When the Sun is low, sunlight crosses the atmosphere at a grazing angle — a path ~10–30× longer than when the Sun is overhead. Along that long path, nearly all the blue is scattered out of the direct beam, leaving the reddish light that reaches your eyes directly. That's why sunsets are red and orange.</p>

          <h3>4. Bonus: why clouds are white</h3>
          <p>Cloud droplets are thousands of times larger than a wavelength, so they scatter all colors about equally. Equal mixing of all colors = white (gray when they get thick enough to shadow each other).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="col" lang="zh-Hans">
          <span class="lang-tag">中文</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>第二级：高中生</h2>
          <p><strong>核心思想：</strong>太阳发出白光——所有可见颜色的混合——而大气把短波（蓝）光散射出光束的程度，远比长波（红）光强得多。</p>

          <h3>1. 空气分子的散射</h3>
          <p>当阳光进入大气层，它会与氮气分子和氧气分子发生相互作用。这些分子比光的波长小得多，因此它们就像微型天线：光的电场使它们的电子振动，而振动的电子向四面八方辐射光。这就是<strong>瑞利散射</strong>。</p>
          <p>散射量近似遵循 <strong>1/λ⁴ 规律</strong>——波长减半，散射的光就增加约 16 倍。因此蓝光（约 450 nm）的散射强度大约是红光（约 650 nm）的 <code>(650/450)⁴ ≈ 4 倍</code>。当你看向太阳以外的任何天空区域时，你看到的几乎全是被头顶上方空气散射<em>进入</em>你视线的蓝光。</p>

          <h3>2. 为什么是蓝色，而不是紫色？</h3>
          <p>紫光（约 400 nm）实际上比蓝光散射得更强。有三个因素共同作用：</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>太阳发出的紫光比绿光或黄光少</strong>（紫外线更少）——太阳光谱的峰值在 500 nm 附近。</li>
            <li><strong>人眼对紫光几乎不敏感</strong>——明视觉灵敏度在 450 nm 处已降到百分之几，在 400 nm 以下不到 1%。</li>
            <li><strong>臭氧吸收紫外线</strong>（320 nm 以下的哈特莱带），在你看到之前就消除了最短的那些波长。</li>
          </ol>
          <p>所有到达你眼睛的散射光叠加在一起，被感知为蓝色。</p>

          <h3>3. 红色的日落</h3>
          <p>当太阳位置较低时，阳光以掠射角穿过大气层——光路比太阳在头顶时长约 10–30 倍。在这条漫长的路径上，几乎所有蓝光都被散射出直射光束，剩下的偏红光直接到达你的眼睛。这就是日落呈红色和橙色的原因。</p>

          <h3>4. 附加题：为什么云是白色的</h3>
          <p>云中的水滴比波长大几千倍，因此它们对所有颜色的散射几乎相同。所有颜色等量混合 = 白色（当云层厚到互相遮挡时则呈灰色）。</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SLIDE 4 · Level 3 ============ -->
  <section class="slide l3" data-label="Level 3 · 物理学博士">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col" lang="en">
          <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>Level 3: A Physics PhD</h2>

          <h3>1. The scattering mechanism</h3>
          <p>For a neutral molecule with (electric) polarizability α in an incident field <strong>E</strong>, the induced dipole is <strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong> (in SI, with α in C·m²/V), and an oscillating dipole radiates with power <code>P = ω⁴|p₀|²/(12πε₀c³) ∝ ω⁴</code>. Dividing by the incident intensity gives the Rayleigh cross section</p>
          <span class="formula">σ(λ) = (8π/3) k⁴ (α/ε₀)² ,&nbsp; k = 2π/λ</span>
          <p>which, via the Lorentz–Lorenz relation <code>σ = (16π³/(3Nλ⁴))(n² − 1)²</code>, depends on the molecular number density N and refractive index. For air at 550 nm, <code>σ ≈ 5.1 × 10⁻³¹ m²</code>, so with <code>N ≈ 2.5 × 10²⁵ m⁻³</code> the scattering mean free path is ~80 km — i.e., each photon has O(10%) probability of a Rayleigh scatter per vertical column, with a vertical optical depth <code>τ(550 nm) ≈ 0.1</code>.</p>
          <p>The differential cross section for an isotropic linear dipole (N₂, O₂) has phase function <code>P(θ) = (3/8π)(1 + cos²θ)</code> — forward–backward symmetric, zero at 90° <em>in the scattering plane</em>, which is why the sky is brightest 90° off the Sun in the <em>perpendicular</em> direction.</p>

          <h3>2. Why blue, and not violet — a radiance budget</h3>
          <p>The sky color is set by the hemispheric integral of scattered radiance, roughly <code>∝ F⊙(λ) · σ(λ) · (scattering geometry) · V(λ)</code>:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Solar spectrum:</strong> F⊙(λ) at 400 nm is only ~30–50% of its value near 500 nm, and UV is suppressed.</li>
            <li><strong>Detector response:</strong> V(λ) peaks at 555 nm, is ~4% at 450 nm and &lt;1% below 400 nm.</li>
            <li><strong>Atmospheric absorption:</strong> the ozone Hartley band removes λ &lt; ~320 nm entirely; the Chappuis band (500–800 nm) weakly tints the direct beam.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The product peaks perceptually in the blue–cyan. The violet that <em>is</em> scattered becomes visible only when the overwhelming blue from other sky regions is removed — at twilight, a faint purplish glow near the Sun (the <strong>Bunsen–Roscoe effect</strong>).</p>

          <h3>3. Sunsets: a numbers exercise</h3>
          <p>At solar zenith angle z, the optical depth scales as <code>τ(z,λ) ≈ τ₀(λ)/cos z</code>. Take z = 85° (sec z ≈ 11.5):</p>
          <ul>
            <li>400 nm: <code>τ₀ ≈ 0.1 × (550/400)⁴ ≈ 0.42 → τ ≈ 4.8</code>, so direct-beam transmission <code>e⁻⁴·⁸ ≈ 0.8%</code></li>
            <li>650 nm: <code>τ₀ ≈ 0.1 × (550/650)⁴ ≈ 0.053 → τ ≈ 0.61</code>, transmission ≈ 55%</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The direct beam is thus bleached of blue — its color temperature drops sharply toward the horizon. Aerosol Mie scattering (extinction <code>∝ λ⁻ᵃ</code>, a ≈ 0–1) then modulates the color of the beam and of the forward-scattered sunset glow; high-altitude dust or volcanic aerosol layers push this to deep reds.</p>

          <h3>4. Polarization and multiple scattering</h3>
          <p>First-order Rayleigh scattering is strongly linearly polarized perpendicular to the scattering plane; for air (depolarization ratio ρ ≈ 0.029, from molecular anisotropy/rotation) the single-scattering maximum degree of polarization at 90° is <code>(1−ρ)/(1+ρ) ≈ 0.94</code>. Observed sky polarization peaks at ~70–80% near 90° from the Sun because of multiple scattering, finite solar disk, and aerosols. Multiple scattering also washes out the blue toward the horizon and mixes in forward-scattered aerosol light, making the horizon whitish — the "blue" of the zenith is essentially single-scatter light, while the horizon is a many-scatter, Mie-contaminated field.</p>

          <h3>5. Common confusions and other worlds</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Clouds:</strong> droplets with size parameter <code>x = 2πr/λ ≫ 1</code> are in the Mie/geometric-optics regime; scattering is nearly wavelength-independent → white. Gray is shadowing (optical depth) and multiple scattering.</li>
            <li><strong>Water's blue</strong> (deep ocean) is a separate mechanism: weak selective <em>absorption</em> of red by H₂O, not Rayleigh scattering. The sky does not get its color from reflecting the ocean.</li>
            <li><strong>Other skies:</strong> Mars is butterscotch by day (Mie scattering by fine CO₂ dust, with a <em>blue</em> sunset — inverted from Earth) and Neptune/Uranus are blue from CH₄ absorption in the UV/visible.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> the sky's blue is first-order Rayleigh scattering from a molecular atmosphere, with the perceived hue determined by the product of the solar spectrum, the 1/λ⁴ cross section, atmospheric absorption, and the eye's spectral sensitivity; the reddish horizon at sunset is the same physics with a sec(z) boost on the short-wavelength optical depth.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="col" lang="zh-Hans">
          <span class="lang-tag">中文</span>
          <h2><span class="dot"></span>第三级：物理学博士</h2>

          <h3>1. 散射机制</h3>
          <p>对于极化率（polarizability）为 α 的中性分子，在入射场 <strong>E</strong> 作用下，感应偶极矩为 <strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong>（SI 单位制中 α 的单位为 C·m²/V），振荡偶极的辐射功率为 <code>P = ω⁴|p₀|²/(12πε₀c³) ∝ ω⁴</code>。除以入射强度即得瑞利散射截面</p>
          <span class="formula">σ(λ) = (8π/3) k⁴ (α/ε₀)² ,&nbsp; k = 2π/λ</span>
          <p>通过洛伦兹—洛伦兹（Lorentz–Lorenz）关系式 <code>σ = (16π³/(3Nλ⁴))(n² − 1)²</code> 可知，它依赖于分子数密度 N 和折射率。对于空气，在 550 nm 处 <code>σ ≈ 5.1 × 10⁻³¹ m²</code>，取 <code>N ≈ 2.5 × 10²⁵ m⁻³</code>，则散射平均自由程约为 80 km——也就是说，每个光子在垂直大气柱中发生一次瑞利散射的概率约为 O(10%)，对应垂直光学厚度 <code>τ(550 nm) ≈ 0.1</code>。</p>
          <p>各向同性线偶极（N₂、O₂）的微分截面具有相函数 <code>P(θ) = (3/8π)(1 + cos²θ)</code>——前后对称，在<em>散射平面内</em> 90° 处为零，这就是为什么在偏离太阳 90°、且垂直于散射平面的方向上天空最亮。</p>

          <h3>2. 为什么是蓝色而不是紫色——辐射度核算</h3>
          <p>天空的颜色由散射辐射度的半球积分决定，大致 <code>∝ F⊙(λ) · σ(λ) ·（散射几何）· V(λ)</code>：</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>太阳光谱：</strong>400 nm 处的 F⊙(λ) 只有 500 nm 附近数值的 30–50%，且紫外部分被压制。</li>
            <li><strong>探测器响应：</strong>V(λ) 在 555 nm 处达到峰值，在 450 nm 处约为 4%，在 400 nm 以下 &lt;1%。</li>
            <li><strong>大气吸收：</strong>臭氧的哈特莱带（Hartley band）完全去除 λ &lt; ~320 nm 的光；查普伊斯带（Chappuis band，500–800 nm）对直射光束有微弱的染色作用。</li>
          </ul>
          <p>该乘积在感知上的峰值位于蓝—青色范围。确实被散射的紫光，只有在其他天空区域压倒性的蓝色被去除后才变得可见——在暮光时刻，太阳附近会泛出淡淡的紫红色光辉（<strong>本生—罗斯科效应</strong>，Bunsen–Roscoe effect）。</p>

          <h3>3. 日落：一道数值题</h3>
          <p>太阳天顶角为 z 时，光学厚度按 <code>τ(z,λ) ≈ τ₀(λ)/cos z</code> 缩放。取 z = 85°（sec z ≈ 11.5）：</p>
          <ul>
            <li>400 nm：<code>τ₀ ≈ 0.1 × (550/400)⁴ ≈ 0.42 → τ ≈ 4.8</code>，因此直射光透射率 <code>e⁻⁴·⁸ ≈ 0.8%</code></li>
            <li>650 nm：<code>τ₀ ≈ 0.1 × (550/650)⁴ ≈ 0.053 → τ ≈ 0.61</code>，透射率 ≈ 55%</li>
          </ul>
          <p>因此直射光束中的蓝色被“漂白”——其色温朝地平线方向急剧下降。气溶胶的米氏散射（消光 <code>∝ λ⁻ᵃ</code>，a ≈ 0–1）随后调制光束的颜色以及向前散射的日落光辉；高层的尘埃或火山气溶胶层会将其推向深红色。</p>

          <h3>4. 偏振与多次散射</h3>
          <p>一级瑞利散射具有强烈的线偏振，偏振方向垂直于散射平面；对空气（去偏振比 ρ ≈ 0.029，源于分子各向异性/转动），单次散射在 90° 处的最大偏振度为 <code>(1−ρ)/(1+ρ) ≈ 0.94</code>。观测到的天空偏振峰值约为 70–80%，出现在偏离太阳约 90° 处，原因是多次散射、有限的日面视大小以及气溶胶。多次散射还会使靠近地平线的蓝色被冲淡，并混入向前散射的气溶胶光，使地平线呈白色——天顶的“蓝色”基本上是单次散射光，而地平线则是经历多次散射、受米氏散射污染的光场。</p>

          <h3>5. 常见误解与其他星球的天空</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>云：</strong>尺寸参数 <code>x = 2πr/λ ≫ 1</code> 的水滴处于米氏/几何光学区域；散射几乎与波长无关 → 白色。灰色来自遮挡（光学厚度）和多次散射。</li>
            <li><strong>水的蓝色</strong>（深海）是另一种机制：H₂O 对红光的微弱选择性<em>吸收</em>，而非瑞利散射。天空的颜色并非来自反射海洋。</li>
            <li><strong>其他天空：</strong>火星白天呈奶油褐色（细 CO₂ 尘埃的米氏散射，而日落时呈<em>蓝色</em>——与地球相反）；海王星/天王星的蓝色来自 CH₄ 在紫外/可见光波段的吸收。</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR（太长不看版）：</strong>天空的蓝色来自分子大气的一级瑞利散射，其感知色相由太阳光谱、1/λ⁴ 散射截面、大气吸收和人眼光谱灵敏度的乘积决定；日落时地平线呈红色，是同样的物理过程，只是短波光学厚度被 sec(z) 因子放大了。</div>
        </div>
